// The property form (docs/design/rfc-properties.md slice 2): named,
// parameterized bundles of proof atoms.
//
// The genericity principle (docs/design/epistemics.md) says the language
// hardcodes no specific epistemic property — the compiler knows nothing
// about "monotonic" or "bounded"; it knows how to check the atoms a
// property's body names. A `property` declaration gives that
// responsibility a syntax: libraries name bundles of the shipped proof
// atoms (invariant clauses, guarded_by clauses), and a `satisfies` clause
// instantiates a bundle on a type by pure substitution. The instantiated
// atoms are discharged by the SAME machinery as hand-written ones; the
// property NAME survives as the type's exported fact.
//
// Judgmental only, generative never: a property can reject a program; it
// can never change what a program does.

// std.verify ships the standard bundles (monotonic, fenced).
import std.verify

// Properties are ordinary declarations — local ones work too. Parameters
// are meta-typed: `field<int>` names an int field of the carrying type,
// `const int` an integer literal. (`field` names a field of any type,
// `type` a concrete class — see verify.fenced.)
property bounded_below(f: field<int>, lo: const int) {
    invariant f >= lo
}

// Instantiation: each clause substitutes the arguments into the body and
// joins the type's own invariant set. `monotonic(self.score)` becomes
// `invariant self.score >= old(self.score)` — a two-state invariant proven
// at every mut-self method boundary and every foreign write site.
// `bounded_below(self.score, 0)` becomes `invariant self.score >= 0`,
// proven at every construction and write site.
//
// Break either one — construct with a negative score, or add a method
// that decreases it — and the compile error blames BOTH sides: the
// property body ("required by property 'monotonic' (defined at ...),
// instantiated with f = self.score") and the failing site in this file.
class Scoreboard satisfies verify.monotonic(self.score), bounded_below(self.score, 0) {
    score: int

    // The guard is what discharges the proofs: on the taken branch the
    // prover knows points > 0, so score + points >= score (monotonic) and
    // score + points >= 0 (bounded) both follow; on the skipped branch
    // nothing changed.
    fn award(mut self, points: int) {
        if points > 0 {
            self.score = self.score + points
        }
    }

    fn total(self) int {
        return self.score
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut board = Scoreboard { score: 0 }
    board.award(10)
    board.award(5)
    print(f"total: {board.total()}")
}
